# sum() had two silent wrong answers, both in the same function, and both in the
# two copies of it that must stay in step: posix_core_sum (src/runtime/runtime_posix.c,
# the one registered on POSIX) and builtin_sum (src/runtime/runtime.c, the WIN32
# registration -- CMakeLists.txt compiles exactly one of them).
#
#   1. A set was read straight out of its storage (`s->i64`, `s->items`) and only
#      when `s->kind == 0 && s->compCount == 0`.  A set literal is a union of i64,
#      items and interval COMPONENTS, so any set written as `1, 2, Z[7~9]` or
#      `Z[1~2], Z[5~6]` failed that gate: both loops were skipped, `ok` stayed 1,
#      and the total stayed at its initial 0.  `len(b)` was 5 and `list(b)` was
#      [1, 2, 7, 8, 9] -- and `sum(b)` was 0.  No exception, exit code 0.
#
#   2. Integers were accumulated in a `double`, so everything above 2^53 was
#      rounded away before it reached push_int: `sum([9007199254740993])` answered
#      9007199254740992.  (On Windows the same total was then cast back to `int`
#      by `push_int(vm, (int)sum)`.)  A value that entered as an integer must not
#      leave as one that silently lost bits.
#
# The fix enumerates a set through vm_set_to_array -- the same path len(), size()
# and list() already use (docs/AUDIT.md §1.10) -- accumulates integers in a 64-bit
# total with a parallel `double` for the moment a float joins the sequence, and
# raises numeric_overflow when the 64-bit total leaves range, which is the rule
# the arithmetic operators follow (docs/AUDIT.md §1.14).
#
# An unbounded set (Z, an interval open into infinity) is refused by
# vm_set_to_array and answers nil -- which is a CHANGE, and a correction: `sum(Z)`
# used to answer 0.  The old gate `s->kind == 0 && s->compCount == 0` failed for
# `Z`, and failing it left `ok` at its initial 1 and the total at its initial 0,
# so an infinite set summed to zero with no error.  `list(Z)` is nil; nil is the
# honest answer and 0 is not.
#
# The defect changes VALUES, never the exit code, so this asserts numbers and pins
# them with both a PASS and a FAIL regex.  The overflow branch exits 1 and so
# cannot live here; it is asserted by contract_test.im, whose try/catch turns the
# throw into a check.
#
# See docs/AUDIT.md §1.15.
b = 1, 2, Z[7~9]
c = Z[1~2], Z[5~6]
d = Z[1~4]
say "sum-ok comp=" + str(sum(b)) + " two=" + str(sum(c)) + " single=" + str(sum(d)) + " big=" + str(sum([9007199254740993])) + " big2=" + str(sum([9007199254740993, 1])) + " plain=" + str(sum([1, 2, 3])) + " float=" + str(sum([1.5, 2])) + " empty=" + str(sum([])) + " bool=" + str(sum([true, 2])) + " inf=" + str(sum(Z))
